"An earlier unit lesson matched each of the seven emotions to an organ it can injure, pairing worry and overthinking with the spleen, and a later lesson described how each emotion disturbs qi's movement specifically — worry described as making qi \"knot\" rather than flow. This lesson connects those two ideas directly to the spleen's actual job, described in the organs unit: transforming food and drink into usable qi and blood, a process that depends on qi moving steadily through the digestive system.": "f29ae71d1890",
"An earlier unit lesson matched each of the seven emotions to an organ it can injure, pairing worry and overthinking with the spleen, and a later lesson described how each emotion disturbs qi's movement specifically — worry described as making qi \"knot\" rather than flow. This lesson connects those two ideas directly to the spleen's actual job, described in the organs unit: transforming food and drink into usable qi and blood, a process that depends on qi moving steadily through the digestive system. Overthinking, in this framework, doesn't just coexist with poor digestion — it interferes with the specific mechanical step the spleen needs to complete its job. Qi that has knotted rather than flowed can't drive the transformation process the way it normally would, which is why sustained worry is so often accompanied by a genuinely reduced appetite, a bloated or heavy feeling after eating even small amounts, and loose, poorly formed digestion — not vague complaints, but a fairly direct physical consequence of a specific organ's qi being obstructed. This is also why advice to simply \"stop worrying\" tends to fail as a fix for digestive trouble caused this way — it treats the emotion as the whole problem rather than the trigger for a specific, physical mechanism. The more useful question this framework suggests isn't \"how do I feel less worried,\" but \"how do I get qi moving through the spleen again\" — sometimes overlapping with reducing worry, but not the same task, and not solved by willpower alone.": "ba3530e0eca7",
